L1: Effects of vitamin D supplementation combined with exercise on glucolipid metabolism in adults: A meta-analysis
L2: iScience (2025)
L3: Yue Feng, Wenxing Zhou, Yanjun Niu
L4: 1 School of Exercise and Health, Shanghai University of Sport, Shanghai 200438, China
L5: 2 Department of Biomedical Engineering, Southern University of Science and Technology, Shenzhen 518055, China
L6: Evidence on vitamin D supplementation combined with exercise for glucolipid metabolism in adults remains inconclusive. This review synthesizes meta-analyses to evaluate their effects. Three databases were searched up to 1 May 2025. This review included randomized controlled trial (RCT) investigating the combination of vitamin D supplementation and exercise in adults, assessing glucolipid metabolism and 25(OH)D as outcomes. Study quality was assessed using the Cochrane Risk of Bias Tool 2. Treatment effects were analyzed using random/fixed-effects models, with Hedges’g for effect sizes. Compared with the control group, the combination of vitamin D supplementation and exercise significantly increased the 25(OH)D level (g: 4.13,  p  = 0.0389) and decreased blood glucose (g: −2.69,  p  = 0.0218), fasting insulin (g: −1.41,  p  < 0.0001), and homeostasis model assessment of insulin resistance (HOMA-IR) (g: −0.64,  p  < 0.0001). The combination of vitamin D supplementation and exercise has been demonstrated to improve glucolipid metabolism more effectively than either intervention alone.
L7: Article
L8: Kinesiology; Human metabolism
L9: Introduction
L10: The global prevalence of chronic metabolic disorders, particularly type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM) and non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD), has escalated into a critical public health problem. Epidemiological data have revealed that 589 million individuals are affected by T2DM worldwide, with projections estimating a rise to 853 million by 2050. 1  NAFLD demonstrates similar pandemic patterns, affecting approximately 30% of the global population and showing persistently increasing incidence rates. 2  These interconnected conditions share pathophysiological hallmarks, including insulin resistance (IR) and dyslipidemia, which synergistically drive disease progression through overlapping molecular pathways. 3  Contemporary management of T2DM and NAFLD primarily involves pharmacological agents targeting metabolic pathways, complemented by lifestyle interventions. However, treatment efficacy remains limited by adverse effects such as weight gain, hypoglycemia, and bone fragility. 4 , 5  Therefore, it is necessary to find sustainable, innovative ways to optimize preventive and therapeutic approaches for chronic metabolic disorders.
L11: Estimates indicate that between 30% and 80% of the global population has vitamin D deficiency. 6  Epidemiological studies demonstrated a correlation between vitamin D deficiency and the development of T2DM, as well as reduced insulin secretion and IR. 7  A deficiency of vitamin D has been demonstrated to result in a decline in the metabolic processes of many cells, thus engendering an elevated risk of developing metabolic diseases, including T2DM. 8  A comprehensive meta-analysis revealed a negative correlation between circulating 25(OH)D levels and the risk of developing T2DM. 9  Furthermore, prospective cohort studies have demonstrated that vitamin D insufficiency accelerates the progression from prediabetes to overt T2DM. 10  Vitamin D supplementation significantly improves glucose homeostasis in T2DM patients by reducing fasting plasma glucose, hemoglobin A1c (HbA1c), and homeostasis model assessment of insulin resistance (HOMA-IR) levels while enhancing insulin sensitivity. 11  There is also evidence to suggest that vitamin D supplementation can ameliorate high-fat diet-induced NAFLD and metabolic disorders. 12  Therefore, vitamin D supplementation may have a role in the prevention and treatment of metabolic health disorders.
L12: Vitamin D protects against diabetes-related complications through its anti-inflammatory and immunomodulatory effects, including the suppression of IR-linked pro-inflammatory cytokines such as interleukin (IL)-1β, IL-6, and tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNF-α). 13 , 14  Vitamin D signaling is mediated by binding of 1,25-(OH)2D 3  to the intracellular vitamin D receptor (VDR). VDR, the specific binding site for 1,25(OH)2D 3 , is widely distributed across over 38 tissues and regulates key genes related to bone health, oxidative stress, chronic diseases, and inflammation. 15  1,25(OH)2D 3  modulates insulin receptor gene transcription, enhances glucose transporter4 (GLUT4)-mediated cellular uptake in skeletal muscle, and inhibits hyperglycemia-induced renin-angiotensin system activation by suppressing renin gene expression at the transcriptional level. 15 , 16 , 17  In addition, exercise was proven effective for improving insulin sensitivity 18  and alleviating IR in T2DM, NAFLD, and other metabolic disease patients, 19  likely by enhancing metabolic flexibility. 20  Exercise enhances muscle-specific VDR transcriptional activation and increases serum 25(OH)D concentrations, potentially through improved vitamin D bioavailability. 21 , 22  Although the individual effects of exercise and vitamin D supplementation on glucolipid metabolism in adults have been well-studied, the potential synergistic benefits of combining these interventions remain uncertain.
L13: Figure 1 PRISMA flow diagram depicting the number of studies included and excluded in the systematic review and meta-analysis
L14: Current evidence remains inconclusive regarding whether vitamin D supplementation combined with exercise has a favorable effect on vitamin D levels and glucolipid metabolism in adults. It remains unknown whether the combination is superior to individual interventions. This review systematically evaluates existing literature on the effects of vitamin D supplementation combined with exercise on vitamin D levels and glucolipid metabolism in adults. Additionally, it provides evidence supporting the role of combined exercise and nutritional supplementation in improving metabolic health among individuals with chronic diseases.  Figure 1  provides an overview of the study method. Figure 1 PRISMA flow diagram depicting the number of studies included and excluded in the systematic review and meta-analysis
L15: Results
L16: Search results
L17: The study selection flowchart is shown in  Figure 1 . Systematic database searches, conducted on May 1, 2025, initially identified 1,950 records. Following the screening of duplicates, 678 records were retained for title and abstract screening. Subsequently, 555 records were excluded, leaving 85 for full text assessment. Finally, 18 RCTs met the inclusion criteria selected for the meta-analysis.
L18: Characteristics of studies
L19: Table 1  summarizes the general characteristics of the included studies. The 18 eligible studies, published between 2013 and 2024, were predominantly conducted in Iran ( n  = 6), Australia ( n  = 3), Estonia ( n  = 2), with the remaining 7 studies from other countries. Among these, all included studies utilized RCT designs. The pooled analysis comprised 1,104 participants across all studies. The intervention groups included the group with the combination of vitamin D supplementation and aerobic exercise (VDE), the vitamin D supplementation group (VD), and the exercise group (exercise). The participants included overweight older women, adults with vitamin D deficiency and NAFLD, overweight and obese adults, overweight women with NAFLD, and individuals with T2DM. The duration of vitamin D supplementation for all participants ranged from 4 weeks to 12 months, with dosages varying from 400 international unit (IU) per day to 8,000 IU per day. Table 1 Basic characteristics of included studies Study Place of study N Study population Age Sex Group Vitamin D supplement (IU) Exercise program Sunlight exposure Duration of trial Outcomes measured VDE VD E C Prusik et al., 2018 23 Poland 114 overweight elderly women 68.00 ± 5.12 F 35 48 – 31 4,000 IU/day complete 12 weeks of Nordic walking training, three times a week, 1 h after breakfast and performed the Nordic walking training at 60–70% intensity of the maximal HR exercise in community settings 12 weeks 25(OH)D, HbA1c, TC, HDL-C, LDL-C, TG, LDL-C/HDL-C, TC/HDL-C, and TC/LDL-C Hoseini et al., 2020 24 Iran 40 VD deficiency and NAFLD 63.10 ± 2.37 F 10 10 10 10 50,000 IU/week aerobic training for 45–60 min at 60%–75% of the maximal HR, 3 days/week running and walking indoor exercise 8 weeks 25(OH)D, FBG, FINS, HOMA-IR, TC, HDL-C, LDL-C, and TG Carrillo et al., 2013 25 United States 23 overweight and obese adults 26.10 ± 4.7 M/F 10 – 13 – 4,000 IU/day resistance training, three times a week, 70%–80%of estimated 1RM winter, la