L20: Risks of bias
L21: Figure 2 The risk of bias assessment for the included studies was conducted using the Cochrane RoB2 tool across five domains Risk levels are indicated as low (pink), unclear (light purple), and high (dark purple).
L22: The methodological quality of the included 18 RCTs was evaluated using the Cochrane RoB2 tool and is presented in  Figure 2 . Of these, 7 studies demonstrated an overall low risk of bias, while 11 studies raised some concerns, primarily regarding randomization procedures. Figure 2 The risk of bias assessment for the included studies was conducted using the Cochrane RoB2 tool across five domains Risk levels are indicated as low (pink), unclear (light purple), and high (dark purple).
L23: Glucose
L24: Figure 3 Effects of vitamin D supplementation, exercise, and combined interventions on glucose levels (A) VDE vs. control. (B) VDE vs. exercise. (C) VDE vs. VD. (D) Exercise vs. control. (E) VD vs. control. VDE, vitamin D supplementation exercise; VD, vitamin D supplementation.
L25: This study conducted a systematic meta-analysis of 11 RCTs to evaluate glucose levels through four pairwise comparisons: VDE vs. control, VDE vs. exercise, VDE vs. VD, exercise vs. control, and VD vs. control ( Figure 3 ). Compared with the control group, glucose levels in the VDE groups were reduced significantly (g: −2.69; 95% confidence interval [CI]: −4.99, −0.39;  p  = 0.0218). The model exhibited a high heterogeneity (I 2  = 91.7%), thereby supporting the employment of a random effects model. Subgroup analysis was conducted based on different interventions. The exercise group (g: −3.02 95% CI: −5.74, −0.30;  p  < 0.0001) showed deceased glucose levels compared to the passive control group. Other group comparisons were not significant ( p  > 0.05). This suggests that combined interventions and single exercise can improve blood glucose. However, the small sample size in this analysis restricts the certainty of these conclusions and calls for further investigation. In the sensitivity analysis of the meta-analysis for each group, when individual studies were successively excluded and under the random effects model, high heterogeneity between studies was observed, but the most of the overall results were relatively stable (see  Figure S1 ). Figure 3 Effects of vitamin D supplementation, exercise, and combined interventions on glucose levels (A) VDE vs. control. (B) VDE vs. exercise. (C) VDE vs. VD. (D) Exercise vs. control. (E) VD vs. control. VDE, vitamin D supplementation exercise; VD, vitamin D supplementation.
L26: Fasting insulin
L27: Figure 4 Effect of vitamin D supplementation combined with exercise on fasting insulin concentration (A) VDE vs. control. (B) VDE vs. exercise. (C) VDE vs. VD. (D) Exercise vs. control. (E) VD vs. control. VDE, vitamin D supplementation exercise.
L28: This study conducted a systematic meta-analysis of 11 RCTs to evaluate fasting insulin levels through four pairwise comparisons: VDE vs. control, VDE vs. exercise, VDE vs. VD, exercise vs. control, and VD vs. control ( Figure 4 ). Compared with the control group, fasting insulin levels in the VDE groups were reduced significantly (g: −1.41; 95%CI: −1.57, −1.26;  p  < 0.0001). The model exhibited a low heterogeneity (I 2  = 28.6%), thereby supporting the employment of a common effects model. Subgroup analysis was conducted based on different interventions. The combined intervention of vitamin D supplementation and exercise demonstrated a significant decrease in fasting insulin levels compared to the exercise group (g: −0.28; 95% CI: −0.55, −0.02;  p  = 0.035,  Figure 4 B). In addition, the VDE group reduced fasting insulin levels better than the VD group (g: −0.65; 95% CI: −0.82, −1.02;  p  < 0.0001). Both the exercise group (g: −1.20; 95% CI: −1.38, −1.02;  p  < 0.0001) and VD group (g: −0.75; 95% CI: −0.95, −0.54;  p  < 0.0001) showed decreased fasting insulin levels compared to the passive control group. This suggests that the combined intervention of vitamin D supplementation and exercise was most effective in reducing fasting insulin levels, while exercise and vitamin D supplementation alone also had a positive effect on fasting insulin levels. Figure 4 Effect of vitamin D supplementation combined with exercise on fasting insulin concentration (A) VDE vs. control. (B) VDE vs. exercise. (C) VDE vs. VD. (D) Exercise vs. control. (E) VD vs. control. VDE, vitamin D supplementation exercise.
L29: HOMA-IR
L30: Figure 5 Effect of vitamin D supplementation combined with exercise on HOMA-IR (A) VDE vs. control. (B) VDE vs. exercise. (C) VDE vs. VD. (D) Exercise vs. control. (E) VD vs. control. VDE, vitamin D supplementation exercise; VD, vitamin D.
L31: This study conducted a systematic meta-analysis of 10 RCTs to evaluate HOMA-IR levels through four pairwise comparisons: VDE vs. control, VDE vs. VD, VDE vs. exercise, exercise vs. control, and VD vs. control ( Figure 5 ). Compared with the control group, HOMA-IR levels in the VDE group were reduced significantly (g: −0.64; 95% CI: −0.79, −0.50;  p  < 0.0001). The model exhibited low heterogeneity (I 2  = 0%), thereby supporting the employment of a common effects model. Subgroup analysis was conducted based on different interventions. The combined intervention of vitamin D supplementation and exercise demonstrated a significant decrease in HOMA-IR levels compared to the VD group (g: −0.23; 95% CI: −0.39, −0.06;  p  = 0.0061). Both the exercise group (g: −0.59; 95% CI: −0.70, −0.49;  p  < 0.0001) and VD group (g: −0.41; 95% CI: −0.58, −0.25;  p  < 0.0001) showed decreased HOMA-IR levels compared to the passive control group. This suggests that the combined intervention of vitamin D supplementation and exercise was the most effective among all interventions in reducing HOMA-IR levels, while exercise and vitamin D supplementation alone also had a positive effect. Figure 5 Effect of vitamin D supplementation combined with exercise on HOMA-IR (A) VDE vs. control. (B) VDE vs. exercise. (C) VDE vs. VD. (D) Exercise vs. control. (E) VD vs. control. VDE, vitamin D supplementation exercise; VD, vitamin D.
L32: Lipid profiles
L33: Figure 6 Effect of vitamin D supplementation combined with exercise on TG (A) VDE vs. control. (B) VDE vs. exercise. (C) VDE vs. VD. (D) Exercise vs. control. (E) VD vs. control. VDE, vitamin D supplementation exercise; VD, vitamin D supplementation.
L34: Figure 7 Effect of vitamin D supplementation combined with exercise on LDL-C (A) VDE vs. control. (B) VDE vs. exercise. (C) VDE vs. VD. (D) Exercise vs. control. (E) VD vs. control. VDE, vitamin D supplementation exercise; VD, vitamin D supplementation.
L35: Figure 8 Effect of vitamin D supplementation combined with exercise on HDL-C (A) VDE vs. control. (B) VDE vs. exercise. (C) VDE vs. VD. (D) Exercise vs. control. (E) VD vs. control. VDE, vitamin D supplementation exercise; VD, vitamin D supplementation.
L36: Ten studies assessed serum total cholesterol ,TC, triglycerides (TG), low-density lipoprotein cholesterol (LDL-C), and high-density lipoprotein cholesterol (HDL-C) concentrations ( Figure 6 ). There was no significant difference in TC between groups (see  Figure S2 ). The vitamin D supplementation demonstrated a significant decrease in LDL-C levels compared to control group (g: −0.76; 95% CI: −1.41, −0.10;  p  = 0.023,  Figure 7 ). The present study found that the combined intervention of vitamin D supplementation and exercise exhibited a decrease in TG levels (g: −0.44; 95% CI: −0.68, −0.20;  p  = 0.0003) and an increase in high-density lipoprotein (HDL) levels (g: 0.66; 95% CI: 0.01, 1.31;  p  = 0.0496,  Figure 8 ) in comparison with the VD group. The remaining groups were found to be non-significant ( p  > 0.05). This suggests that the combined intervention was more effective in improving fat metabolism compared with single VD supplementation. Figure 6 Effect of vitamin D supplementation combined with exercise on TG (A) VDE vs. control. (B) VDE vs. exercise. (C) VDE vs. VD. (D) Exercise vs. control. (E) VD vs. control. VDE, vitamin D supplementation exercise; VD, vitamin D supplementation. Figure 7 Effect of vitamin D supplementation combined with exercise on LDL-C (A) VDE vs. control. (B) VDE vs. exercise. (C) VDE vs. VD. (D) Exercise vs. control. (E) VD vs. control. VDE, vitamin D supplementation exercise; VD, vitamin D supplementation. Figure 8 Effect of vitamin D supplementation combined with exercise on HDL-C (A) VDE vs. control. (B) VDE vs. exercise. (C) VDE vs. VD. (D) Exercise vs. control. (E) VD vs. control. VDE, vitamin D supplementation exercise; VD, vitamin D supplementation.
L37: 25(OH)D
L38: Figure 9 Effects of vitamin D supplementation, exercise, and combined interventions on 25(OH)D levels (A) VDE vs. control. (B) VDE vs. exercise. (C) VDE vs. VD. (D) Exercise vs. control. (E) VD vs. control. VDE, vitamin D supplementation exercise; VD, vitamin D supplementation.
L39: This study conducted a systematic meta-analysis of 15 RCTs to evaluate 25(OH)D levels through four pairwise comparisons: VDE vs. control, VDE vs. exercise, VDE vs. VD, exercise vs. control, and VD vs. control ( Figure 9 ). The present analysis incorporated data from 122 participants in the VDE groups and 120 in the control groups. Results showed that vitamin D supplementation combined with exercise significantly improved 25(OH)D levels compared to passive control conditions (g: 4.13; 95% CI: 0.21, 8.04;  p  = 0.0389). The model exhibited a high heterogeneity (I 2  = 94.4%), thereby supporting the employment of a random effects model. Subgroup analysis was conducted based on different interventions. The combined intervention of vitamin D supplementation and exercise demonstrated a significant increase in 25(OH)D levels compared to the exercise group (g: 3.65; 95% CI: 2.11, 5.18;  p  < 0.0001). Compared with the VD group, the VDE group showed a tendency to increase 25(OH)D levels (g: 2.42; 95% CI: −0.26, 5.11;  p  = 0.077). Both the exercise group (g: 5.45; 95% CI: 4.48, 6.41;  p  < 0.0001) and VD group (g: 11.07; 95% CI: 4.94, 17.20;  p  = 0.0004) showed increased 25(OH)D levels compared to the passive control group. This suggests a synergistic effect of the combined intervention. In the sensitivity analysis of the meta-analysis for each group, when individual studies were successively excluded and under the random effects model, high heterogeneity between studies was observed, but the overall results were relatively stable (see  Figure S3 ). Follow-up studies need to focus on the sources of variation between studies, as there was a high degree of heterogeneity between analyses. Figure 9 Effects of vitamin D supplementation, exercise, and combined interventions on 25(OH)D levels (A) VDE vs. control. (B) VDE vs. exercise. (C) VDE vs. VD. (D) Exercise vs. control. (E) VD vs. control. VDE, vitamin D supplementation exercise; VD, vitamin D supplementation.
L40: Discussion
L41: The current study included 18 RCTs to comprehensively evaluate the efficacy of the combined intervention of vitamin D supplementation and exercise on glucolipid metabolism and 25(OH)D levels in adults. This study shows that the combination of vitamin D supplementation and exercise increased 25(OH)D levels and provided significant benefits in improving glycemic control in adults, as indicated by marked reductions in glucose, HOMA-IR and fasting insulin levels. Regarding lipid metabolism, the combined intervention of vitamin D supplementation and exercise reduced TG levels and increased HDL-C levels. We conducted additional subgroup analyses based on the characteristics of the intervention measures to better understand the differences between the combined intervention and the single intervention in terms of 25(OH)D levels and glucolipid metabolism outcomes.
L42: This study demonstrates that, compared to the passive control group and the single exercise group, the combined intervention of vitamin D supplementation and exercise elicits a statistically significant increase in 25(OH)D levels. These findings suggest that the combined intervention strategy likely exerts a synergistic effect, outperforming single interventions in increasing 25(OH)D levels. This synergistic impact is consistent with previous research evidence. Liu et al.'s research has indicated that higher 25(OH)D levels are associated with a lower risk of biological aging, further suggesting that the combination of 25(OH)D and physical activity confers an enhanced protective effect especially among middle-aged or young individuals. 41  The interplay between vitamin D and exercise is a complex and multifaceted relationship, involving various cellular and molecular signaling pathways. Exercise can influence the metabolism of vitamin D by increasing the production of 1,25-(OH) 2 D, the bioactive form of vitamin D, through the upregulation of the enzyme 1α-hydroxylase in skeletal muscle and other tissues. 42 , 43  The kidney converts 25-(OH)D to 1,25-(OH) 2 D, which binds to VDR to regulate expression of vitamin D responsive genes. 44  Enhanced activation of vitamin D mitigates reactive oxygen species production, fortifies antioxidant defenses against oxidative stress, and upregulates the expression of genes crucial for muscle growth, repair, and mitochondrial biogenesis, thereby enhancing exercise performance. Therefore, the combined intervention of vitamin D supplementation and exercise may have a superimposed effect in improving 25(OH)D levels.
L43: The results of this study demonstrate that single vitamin D supplementation increases 25(OH)D levels and improves glycemic control and insulin sensitivity. This may be related to the fact that 1,25(OH) 2 D activates VDR. Vitamin D can improve glucose homeostasis by enhancing insulin secretion, reducing inflammation, reducing autoimmunity, preserving beta cell mass, and sensitizing insulin action. 43 , 45 , 46  Multiple mechanisms underlie vitamin D’s regulation of insulin synthesis and secretion. On the one hand, 1,25(OH) 2 D binds to the VDR to induce the expression of genes critical for glucose transport, insulin secretion, and β-cell growth. 47 , 48  On the other hand, vitamin D could enhance glucose homeostasis via its modulation of intracellular calcium level. 45  1,25(OH) 2 D activates VDR to regulate voltage-gated calcium channel expression to improve insulin secretion. 49  Furthermore, vitamin D’s anti-inflammatory and immunomodulatory effects may inhibit the activation of nuclear factor kappa B, thus relieving islet dysfunction and improving glucose metabolism. 50
L44: The present study demonstrates that single exercise significantly elevates 25(OH)D levels and improves glycemic control and insulin sensitivity compared to the passive control group. Our previous study confirmed that exercise significantly increased 25(OH)D levels in vitamin D-deficient mice, which may be associated with exercise-induced increases in VDR expression. 51  The beneficial impacts of exercise and vitamin D supplementation are time-dependent. Our findings indicate that 25(OH)D levels and lipid metabolism parameters reverted to baseline values six months after discontinuing training and vitamin D supplementation. This feature was also observed in our recent study. 33  In the exercise group combined with vitamin D supplementation, the effect on lipid metabolism lasted until 12 weeks of follow-up, but not in the exercise group alone or in the vitamin D supplementation group. In addition, these improved effects were influenced by baseline 25(OH)D levels, vitamin D supplementation dose, and exercise dose. However, the small number of studies in this study did not allow for valid subgroup analyses.
L45: This study found that vitamin D supplementation combined with exercise reduced glucose, HOMA-IR and fasting insulin levels, and that the combined effect was superior to a single intervention. This may be due to the fact that vitamin D supplementation combined with exercise significantly increases 25(OH)D levels, thus providing an ample substrate for the synthesis of 1,25(OH) 2 D, which activates the VDR, increases insulin receptor expression, and consequently improves insulin sensitivity. Evidence supporting this perspective has been reported in studies where participants underwent 8 weeks of 50,000 IU weekly vitamin D supplementation combined with 75% HRmax aerobic training and showed increased serum 25(OH)D levels. This intervention also significantly reduced fasting insulin, fasting blood glucose, HbA1c, and HOMA-IR, indicating improved glycemic control and insulin sensitivity. 27  Thus, exercise combined with vitamin D supplementation may have a stacked effect in improving metabolic health.
L46: The findings of this study revealed that, compared with the vitamin D group, the combined intervention of vitamin D supplementation and exercise not only decreased TG levels but also increased HDL levels. This suggests that combined interventions are more effective than supplementation alone. In animal experiments, both exercise and vitamin D supplementation effectively improved the body composition, lipid profiles, and spexin levels in T2D rats. Specifically, significant reductions were observed in TC, TG, and LDL-C, while HDL-C levels increased substantially. The combination of exercise and vitamin D supplementation yielded the most pronounced improvements, suggesting a potential synergistic effect between exercise and vitamin D supplementation. 52  This finding is consistent with the present study. Vitamin D function is closely linked to the expression of VDR, which facilitates the absorption and utilization of vitamin D; reduced VDR expression in adipose and muscle tissues has been observed in diabetic models, while swimming exercise was shown to reverse these reductions and improve metabolic parameters such as serum glucose, insulin, HOMA-IR, TC, TG, and LDL-C after 4 weeks. 53  Serum 25(OH)D levels were negatively correlated with serum glucose, insulin, HOMA-IR, TC, TG, and LDL-C, while positively correlated with HDL-C and VDR expression. Our previous studies also confirmed that exercise promotes VDR expression, and disruptions in the vitamin D/VDR pathway are associated with metabolic dysfunction. 51  Exercise enhances lipid metabolism via multiple pathways. It activates hormone-sensitive lipase and adipose triglyceride lipase, promoting lipolysis and releasing free fatty acids that are transported to the liver for β-oxidation. This process decreases hepatic TG production and elevates HDL-C levels. 54  Moderate aerobic exercise appears to mitigate these consequences by regulating vitamin D concentrations. 53  Notably, the combined benefits of vitamin D supplementation and exercise may be more pronounced in individuals with impaired insulin sensitivity and vitamin D deficiency but show limited impact in those with sufficient vitamin D levels. 55
L47: Conclusion
L48: Vitamin D supplementation combined with exercise regulates lipid metabolism and improves IR more effectively than either exercise or vitamin D supplementation alone. Current studies suggest that the combination of vitamin D supplementation and exercise may serve as an effective adjuvant intervention for blood glucose control and dyslipidemia, thereby providing valuable guidance for the clinical application of vitamin D supplementation. This approach is cost-effective for individuals with vitamin D deficiency and IR.
L49: Limitations of the study
L50: There are still some limitations in the current study, and the research results need to be interpreted with caution. Firstly, although the present meta-analysis provides preliminary evidence, due to the limited sample sizes of the included studies, some of them lack sufficient details for an accurate qualitative assessment. Secondly, this analysis lacks an examination of the dosage of vitamin D and exercise dosage (e.g., frequency, duration, intensity), which restricts the in-depth understanding of the comprehensive impact of the combined intervention regimen. However, this is mainly because there is very little data from randomized controlled trials. Finally, the use of baseline medications by the participants may have masked the subtle effects of vitamin D. The potential impacts of anti-diabetic medications, insulin therapy, and medications for dyslipidemia on vitamin D treatment also need to be further considered. The results of this study can help formulate the optimal nutritional and exercise intervention plans for populations with vitamin D deficiency or IR. Subsequently, the combination of vitamin D supplementation and exercise can be incorporated into treatment plans for disease prevention and management. In the future, it is also possible to explore the optimal dosage of vitamin D supplementation and exercise patterns for different age groups and populations with various underlying diseases. Meanwhile, in-depth research can be conducted on the impact of genetic differences on the intervention effect to achieve precise intervention. Additionally, long-term follow-up studies can be carried out to evaluate the long-term effects and safety of the combined intervention, providing a more solid evidence base for clinical practice and public health policies, and promoting the standardized application of this intervention model on a larger scale.
