L67: Experimental model and study participant details
L68: Inclusion and exclusion criteria
L69: The inclusion criteria were: (1) adults (≥ 18 years) ; (2) RCT design; (3) The primary outcome was glucose metabolism (glucose, fasting insulin, HOMA-IR, HbA1c), lipid metabolism(TC, TG, HDL-C, LDL-C), and the secondary outcome was 25(OH)D; (4) peer-reviewed articles published in English; (5) comparison with the groups without the intervention of vitamin D supplementation combined with exercise (e.g., placebo, exercise group, vitamin D supplementation group. Exclusion criteria were: (1) the dose of vitamin D supplementation was not clearly described (2) studies with data that could not be extracted or were not clearly reported. Two investigators (Y.F. and W.X.Z.) independently conducted the entire records selection processes, which involved title/abstract screening and full-text assessment using a standardized form. Discrepancies were settled via discussion and, if necessary, arbitration by a third researcher (Y.J.N).
L70: Method details
L71: This systematic review and meta-analysis followed the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) guidelines. 20  The study was registered in advance with the International Prospective Register of Systematic Reviews (PROSPERO;CRD42023412614).
L72: The systematic literature search was initiated on August 1, 2023 and iteratively updated through May 1, 2025, employing MEDLINE (PubMed), EMBASE, and Web of Science databases for comprehensive retrieval of relevant publications. The search consisted of the following terms and Medical Subject Heading (MeSH) terms (and their respective related terms) associated with each other: (vitamin D OR VD OR vitamin D2 OR vitamin D3 OR cholecalciferol) AND (exercise OR sport OR aerobic exercise OR resistance training OR training) AND (insulin resistance OR glucose metabolism OR glucose OR FBG OR FINS OR INS OR insulin sensitivity OR HOMA-IR OR insulin secretion OR lipid metabolism OR TC OR TG OR LDL-C OR HDL-C OR Total Cholesterol OR Triglyceride OR Low-Density Lipoprotein Cholesterol OR High-Density Lipoprotein Cholesterol OR 25-hydroxyvitamin D OR 25(OH)D) AND (randomized controlled trial OR controlled clinical trial OR controlled trial OR RCT).
L73: Two independent reviewers (Y.F. and W.X.Z.) assessed the methodological quality of each eligible RCT using the revised Risk of Bias 2 (RoB 2) tool. The assessment covered five key domains: (1) randomization process, (2) deviations from intended interventions, (3) missing outcome data, (4) measurement of outcomes, and (5) selection of reported results. Based on the RoB 2 criteria, studies were categorized as “low risk of bias” (i.e., all domains judged as low risk), “some concerns” (i.e., at least one domain with some concerns), or “high risk of bias” (i.e., at least one domain with high risk or multiple domains with some concerns). Any discrepancies were resolved through consensus-based discussion.
L74: Quantification and statistical analysis
L75: Two independent researchers (Y.F. and W.X.Z.) screened and extracted data from each included study. In cases where discrepancies arose between their results, a third researcher served as an arbitrator to resolve the differences. For all included studies, the researchers extracted the following basic information: author, year, country, sample size, sample characteristics (including age and sex), grouping methods (control group, vitamin D supplementation group, exercise group, vitamin D supplementation combined exercise group), vitamin D supplementation details (dosage and frequency), exercise-related parameters (mode, intensity, and frequency), and outcome measures. The outcome measures included fasting glucose, fasting insulin, HOMA-IR, lipid profiles (TC, TG, LDL-C, HDL-C), and 25(OH)D, all of which were assessed before and after the intervention.
L76: All meta-analytic calculations were performed using R software (version 4.4.2) with the “meta” and “metafor” packages 56 , 57 . Post-intervention changes for each outcome were combined using either a fixed-effect or random-effects model, and a forest plot was generated to visualize the results. Effect sizes (Hedges’g with 95% confidence intervals) for outcome comparisons between the intervention and control groups were calculated from post-intervention means, standard deviations (SD). When unavailable, these were derived from alternative statistics (e.g., standard errors, confidence intervals, or p-values). For studies with shared control groups, we halved the control group sample size to avoid unit-of-analysis errors. The magnitude of the effect size was categorized as small (0.2≤g<0.5), medium (0.5≤g<0.8), or large (g≥0.8), with statistical significance at α < 0.05 (two-tailed). Quantitative synthesis was performed only when a minimum of three studies reported available data for the outcome. Study heterogeneity was assessed using  I 2  statistic (low: <25%, moderate: 25-50%, and high: >50%). 58  Subgroup analysis was conducted to assess heterogeneity and compare different interventions, specifically to determine whether vitamin D supplementation combined with exercise is more effective than exercise alone or vitamin D supplementation alone.
